DRACO.md: rewrite against the tree, and say which docs are not

DRACO.md is the file Claude Code auto-loads through the CLAUDE.md symlink, so it is the
input to every session. It described an architecture that had not existed for months:
the *Instance triad, `typeInstance` vals, `TypeElement extends Primal[String]`, Generator
owning type loading, "no elementTypeNames in JSON", a Key Files table listing files that
are gone and omitting TypeLoader, DefinitionPath, Holon, Assembly, Problem and the four
validation rules. Nothing about the .drake surface — 91 files, an emitter, a parser and
two gates — appeared anywhere in it.

Measured rather than asserted: `TypeInstance`/`DomainInstance`/`RuleInstance`/
`typeInstance` occurred 26 times in the file and in ZERO Scala sources.

The clearest evidence that this was worth doing first is internal. Two artifacts in this
repo describe the same fact. MEMORY.md says "dracoType/domainType/ruleType/actorType
kind-vals; *Instance traits deleted" — correct. DRACO.md said the opposite, for months.
Same author, same sessions. Memory is updated as part of doing the work; DRACO.md was
updated when someone remembered. Diligence is not the differentiator, triggering is.

WHAT THE FILE IS NOW. Operating rules first, because those are what do not change with
the architecture: no sbt/git, definitions move as a trio (JSON + .drake + .scala), the
lazy-val discipline, what each of the three gates pins and therefore what a failure
means, the four-artifact split (memory / issues / git-record / journal), the commit
message and pathspec mechanics learned the hard way this week, and the rule that
model-authored prose is not Dev's intent.

Then a compact ORIENTATION section, every claim checked against the tree on 2026-08-15.
It is deliberately short: architecture detail belongs in README once README is corrected.
But README is itself stale, so pointing at it and stopping would have made the next
session worse, not better. Hence §5, which states plainly which neighbouring documents
are unreliable — README, AGENTS.md, GETTING_STARTED, CHANGELOG — and which are not
(drake.dlt is current and authoritative).

TWO RULES RECONCILED. The old file instructed "file an issue for deferred work"; memory
carries Dev's restraint rule. Those contradicted each other and I was subject to both.
Dev's call, now written down: a new issue requires explicit approval every time,
deferred work is surfaced in conversation, comments on an existing issue are ungated.

A RETIRED-NAMES LIST, which is the part meant to outlive this commit. Every major drift
the review found was the same failure: a type was removed and the prose kept the name.
That is a checkable property with no false positives — unlike a general "every symbol
must resolve" lint, which I tested and measured at 115 non-resolving tokens out of 165,
almost all ordinary English or host types. The list is the seed of that gate.

NOT DONE, deliberately. README, GETTING_STARTED and CHANGELOG each need their own pass.
AGENTS.md should become a symlink to this file, but it is a diverged older copy and
replacing it deletes tracked content, so it waits for Dev.
